Ladybird slices an SVG border image at its natural size, or at the
border image area if it has none, and paints each piece from a tile it
renders the whole image into at one scale, offset so that the piece
fills it, then stretches and repeats. Blink, WebKit, and Gecko all
slice it at that same size: Blink through StyleFetchedImage::ImageSize()
and SVGImageForContainer::ConcreteObjectSize(), WebKit through
Style::BorderImageSizing, whose default object size is the border image
area, and Gecko through nsImageRenderer::ComputeConcreteSize() in
nsCSSBorderImageRenderer. Each then draws a piece by scaling the vector
content into it per axis: Blink in SVGImage::DrawInternal(), WebKit in
SVGImage::drawImage(), and Gecko in ClippedImage::DrawSingleTile(). To
repeat a piece, Blink records the scaled piece as a paint shader, and
WebKit’s SVGImage::drawPattern() rasterizes the whole image before it
patterns the piece. Rendering at one scale and stretching the tile is
Ladybird’s own way: Its nested display lists can’t be scaled on each
axis separately, as those engines’ drawing contexts can.
